<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<opml version="2.0"><head><title>Campaign clocks and threads</title></head><body>
<outline text="# Campaign clocks and threads">
  <outline text="The pressure layer of a solo campaign: clocks that fill as danger builds, and threads that tick on a date. A clock is the built-in `[o /6]` ring over checkbox segments (or `[o 3/6]` to click by hand), a thread is a point with a `{date due: today+3}`, and one saved search pulls every open thread at once. See campaign-clocks.md for the walkthrough."/>
  <outline text="## Clocks (fill a segment, the ring ticks)">
    <outline text="A clock is one point whose text carries an `[o /6]` clock. It counts the checkbox children below it and fills a quarter-ring gauge as you check them. Check a segment and the ring climbs. Fill the last one and the clock is full: something happens in the fiction. (Write `[o 3/6]` with a number instead to click the ring by hand, no children needed.)"/>
    <outline text="The Ashguild moves against you [o /6]">
      <outline text="- [x] a paid informant marks your safehouse"/>
      <outline text="- [x] the guild posts watchers on your street"/>
      <outline text="- [ ] a bravo shadows you across the market"/>
      <outline text="- [ ] your fence stops answering the door"/>
      <outline text="- [ ] they name the price on your head"/>
      <outline text="- [ ] the raid comes at dawn"/>
    </outline>
    <outline text="The flood behind the sea-gate [o /6]">
      <outline text="- [x] a hairline crack weeps at the mortar"/>
      <outline text="- [x] the low cells take an inch of water"/>
      <outline text="- [x] the night watch reports a groan in the stone"/>
      <outline text="- [ ] the gate warden calls for engineers"/>
      <outline text="- [ ] the tide runs a hand higher than charted"/>
      <outline text="- [ ] the sea-gate gives"/>
    </outline>
    <outline text="A manual clock carries its own number and has no segment boxes: click the ring to advance one, Shift-click to step it back. The harbormaster's patience [o 2/6]"/>
    <outline text="A percent clock reads the same segments as a bar: your cover story holds [%]">
      <outline text="- [x] the harbormaster still buys your name"/>
      <outline text="- [ ] a rival notices you asking the wrong questions"/>
      <outline text="- [ ] two stories you told no longer agree"/>
      <outline text="- [ ] someone who knew the old you walks in"/>
    </outline>
  </outline>
  <outline text="## Threads (a due date makes it tick)">
    <outline text="A thread is an open question with a clock or a deadline. Tag it (a hash followed by the word thread) and give it a `{date due: today+3}`, and it becomes a due-date chip that shows up in the agenda, so the campaign nudges you when the thread is ready to pay off. The three threads below carry that tag, which is what the search further down finds."/>
    <outline text="Who set the fire at the granary? #thread {date due: today+3}">
      <outline text="Ask the oracle when a lead turns up: {Yes 6 | No 6}. On a yes, advance the clock a segment."/>
      <outline text="Progress toward an answer [o /4]">
        <outline text="- [x] a witness half-remembers a hooded figure"/>
        <outline text="- [ ] the figure's cloak bore a guild pin"/>
        <outline text="- [ ] the pin traces to a name"/>
        <outline text="- [ ] the name has a reason to want you gone"/>
      </outline>
    </outline>
    <outline text="The debt to the moneylender comes due #thread {date due: today+7}">
      <outline text="Roll what they send when the day arrives: {a polite letter | a broken window | two large men | a summons to the counting-house}."/>
    </outline>
    <outline text="The comet the star-priests fear #thread {date due: 2026-12-21}">
      <outline text="A slow thread on a fixed date. It waits in the agenda under Later until its week comes around."/>
    </outline>
  </outline>
  <outline text="## Pull every open thread (a saved search)">
    <outline text="To see the whole pressure board at once, search for the thread tag in the search box. It lists every thread point across the document. Star that search to save it, and it becomes a one-click chip you can re-run any session. Add is:todo or due:week to narrow it to what is live right now."/>
    <outline text="This live pill embeds the same search and updates as you play: {query: #thread}"/>
    <outline text="Narrow to what is on the clock this week by adding `due:week` to that search"/>
  </outline>
  <outline text="## How to run it">
    <outline text="Tick a clock: check one of its segment boxes. The `[o /6]` ring beside the clock's title climbs on its own (or click the ring itself on a manual `[o 2/6]` clock, the harbormaster's patience above). Fill the last box and read the fiction that the full clock triggers."/>
    <outline text="Start a thread: make a point for the open question, tag it with the thread tag, and set a date with `{date due: today+3}` (or a real date like `{date due: 2026-12-21}`). Open the agenda from the toolbar calendar to see it tick closer."/>
    <outline text="Work the board: search the thread tag to list every open thread and the segments under it, star it to keep the chip, and click any result to jump to that thread and advance its clock. Nothing here is new syntax: a clock is the built-in [o N/M] ring, a thread is a dated point, and the board is a saved search."/>
  </outline>
</outline>
</body></opml>
